\section{C4 Training Data Control}
\label{app:c4_control}

To test whether WikiText-103's narrow domain drives the knowledge-task regression (\S\ref{sec:experiments}), we train the KL predictor on C4~\cite{raffel2020exploring} (diverse web text) under identical hyperparameters (1000 steps, 30\% uniform, s42).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{C4-trained vs.\ WikiText-trained KL predictor (LLaMA-3.1-8B, 30\% uniform, s42).}
\label{tab:c4_control}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Training Data & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K & HS / PIQA \\
\midrule
WikiText-103 & 8.80 & 40.78 & 68.59 & 40.6 & 7.35 & 69.4 / 75.5 \\
C4 & 11.55 & 43.09 & 68.67 & 39.95 & 4.09 & 69.24 / 74.86 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The C4-trained predictor achieves comparable MMLU (40.0\% vs.\ 40.6\%) but worse GSM8K (4.1\% vs.\ 7.4\%) and higher PPL (11.55 vs.\ 8.80), despite training on more diverse text.
This rules out training data composition as the primary cause of knowledge-task regression and supports the hypothesis that forward KL's mode-covering property structurally under-protects rare reasoning-critical activations. % exp_id: c4_control_eval

